.TH CGHANAFUDA 6 "November 2026" "cggames 1.4.0"
.SH NAME
cghanafuda \- flower card games: Koi-Koi and Oicho-Kabu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cghanafuda
.RI [ "toolkit options" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cghanafuda
plays games against the computer with hanafuda, the Japanese flower
cards: twelve months of four cards each, the month's flower on every
one.
The games are Koi-Koi and Oicho-Kabu; the Game menu chooses which, and
the title bar names it.
Choosing the other game in the middle of one asks whether to play it
now, or next.
.B Rules...
in the Help menu tells the rules of the game being played.
The cards are drawn to fit the window, at whatever size it is.
.SH KOI-KOI
The rules are Nintendo's, as it gives them for its own cards; the
options below add some common house rules.
Some cards are worth more than others: the five brights, the animals,
the ribbons, and the plain cards.
.PP
For the first round, each player turns a card, and the one with the
earlier month deals, or in the same month the one with the card worth
more.
Eight cards are laid face up on the field, and each player is dealt
eight; the rest are the stock.
The dealer plays first.
In turn, a player plays a card from the hand onto the field: if a field
card is of the same month, the player takes both; if none is, the card
stays on the field.
Then the top card of the stock is turned and played the same way.
With two field cards of the month, the player takes one of them; with
three, all of them.
.PP
Click a card in your hand: the field cards of its month light up.
Click it again to play it, or click the field card it is to take.
When a card turned from the stock could take either of two, click the
one it takes.
The table is laid out as a real one: the computer's hand across it,
face down, the stock at the left of the field with a card turned from
it below, and the cards each of you has taken in front of you, small,
by kind.
.SS Yaku
Cards taken score in sets, yaku:
.TP
.B Goko
the five brights: 10 points;
.B Shiko,
four brights without the rain man: 8;
.B Ame-shiko,
four with him: 7;
.B Sanko,
three without him: 5.
.TP
.B Ino-shika-cho
the boar, the deer and the butterflies: 5, and 1 more for each other
animal.
.TP
.B Akatan
the three poem ribbons: 5;
.B Aotan,
the three blue ribbons: 5;
each 1 more for each other ribbon; both, 10, and 1 more for each other
ribbon.
.TP
.B Hanami-zake, Tsukimi-zake
the curtain, or the moon, with the sake cup: 5 each.
.TP
.B Tane, Tan, Kasu
five animals, five ribbons, or ten plain cards: 1, and 1 more for each
card beyond.
Tane doesn't count with Ino-shika-cho, which counts the animals itself,
nor Tan with Akatan or Aotan.
.SS Koi-koi
A player who makes a yaku can stop, and win the round with its points,
or call koi-koi and play on for more.
After a koi-koi, the next yaku either player makes ends the round: if
it is the other player's, they win it, and their points count double.
Seven points or more count double too.
When the cards run out before a yaku, or after a koi-koi before
another, no one scores.
A hand that is dealt all four cards of a month, or four pairs, wins at
once, for 6 points; if both hands are, no one scores.
The winner of a round deals the next, and with no winner the deal
passes; a game is three, six or twelve rounds.
.SS Learning
While you are learning, the game can help:
.TP
.B Lights and names
The cards in your hand that can take one from the field are lit, and
the status bar names the card under the pointer, its month, its kind
and the yaku it is part of.
When you make a yaku, the question says what the computer would do in
your place.
These can be turned off in Options.
.TP
.B Game, Hint
Raises the card the computer would play in your place, with what it
would take marked; or marks the card a card drawn would take.
.TP
.B Help, Yaku
A chart of the yaku, each with its cards: yours as they are, the
computer's dark, and those still to be won faded; and how many of them
each of you has.
It follows the game.
.TP
.B Help, Cards
A chart of the cards, a month at a time, with those taken darker.
.SH OICHO-KABU
A banking game, like baccarat, against the computer as dealer, with the
forty cards of January to October, each worth its month: January 1 to
October 10.
A hand is worth the last digit of its sum: 9 is best, and 10 or 20 is
nothing.
.PP
The dealer lays a card face up on each of four lanes, and one face down
for itself.
Click a lane to bet a chip on it, up to ten chips a round, and click
it with the right button to take one back; then click the stock, at the
left of the dealer's card, to deal.
Each lane gets a second card, and those not bet on a third.
In turn, a lane bet on that makes 3 or less takes a third card; one
that makes 4 to 6, or 9, is lit, and takes one if you click the stock,
or stands if you click the lane; one that makes 7 or 8 stands.
Then the dealer turns up its card and takes a second, and a third if it
makes 5 or less.
.PP
A lane worth more than the dealer's hand wins its bet, and one worth
less loses it.
A tie stands off, the bet kept, as the computer always deals; or it
goes to the dealer, as when the deal goes round the table.
.TP
.B Arashi
three of a month: wins three times the bet, whatever the dealer holds,
or the dealer's takes three times; if both have it, the higher wins.
.TP
.B Kuppin
the dealer's first two cards, 9 and 1: beats every lane but arashi.
.TP
.B Shippin
a lane's first two cards, 4 and 1: beats every hand of the dealer's
but arashi and kuppin, and stands.
.PP
You start with fifty chips; a game is three, six or twelve rounds, or
ends when the chips are gone.
.PP
With
.B Learning
on, the status bar names the card under the pointer and its worth, and
says what its lane or the dealer makes.
.B Game, Hint
(Ctrl+H) plays the deal out many times over from the cards you can't
see, the stock and the dealer's face down card: while you bet, it marks
the lane that does best and says how often it wins and loses; when a
lane may draw, it marks the stock or the lane, whichever does better,
with both chances.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B Cards
Traditional, the first choice, with black leaves, or Light, with green
leaves and brown wood; and either with a red border and back, the
first choice, or a black one.
.TP
.B Length
Three, six or twelve rounds, from the next game.
.TP
.B Table
Green felt, the first choice, or a tatami mat edged left and right in
blue cloth.
.TP
.B Koi-Koi
Whether the sake cup counts as a plain card too, as Nintendo's rules
have it; whether the rain man spoils the viewing yaku; whether seven
points or more count double; whether winning after the other player
has called koi-koi doubles the points; whether koi-koi may be called
again, each time a player has more points, and the round ends only
when one stops; and whether a round no one makes a yaku in gives the
dealer 6 points, the dealer's privilege, with the deal staying.
.TP
.B Oicho-Kabu
Whether a tie goes to the dealer.
.TP
.B Learning
Whether Koi-Koi lights the cards that match, names the card under the
pointer, and says what the computer would do.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cggames/cghanafuda.conf
The options and the window's size, or
.I ~/.config/cggames/cghanafuda.conf
without the variable.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/cggames/cghanafuda/cards
The cards' pictures, a set to each SVG:
.I traditional.svg
and
.IR light.svg .
.SH AUTHORS
cghanafuda was written for CGTK, and its cards were drawn for it.
